    <string name="help_tv_title">My TV doesn’t show up</string>
    <string name="help_tv_body">Turn the TV on and use the same Wi-Fi as the phone (not a guest network). Turn off any VPN. Still nothing? Add it by its IP address (Choose a TV → Add a TV by IP address).</string>
    <string name="help_freeze_title">The video freezes when I skip ahead</string>
    <string name="help_freeze_body">The app serving the video was put to sleep by Android: let it run in the background (status below). With a torrent, the part you jump to must also be downloaded first: give it a few seconds.</string>
    <string name="help_format_title">“File not supported” on the TV</string>
    <string name="help_format_body">The TV can’t read this format: often HEVC/x265 (the format of many phone videos), DTS sound or MKV on older TVs. Try another source or quality; for phone videos, film in “H.264” or “Most compatible” mode.</string>
    <string name="help_subs_title">No subtitles</string>
    <string name="help_subs_body">Universal TV Cast finds subtitles automatically from the video name and picks your language. On Google Cast TVs they appear directly. On other TVs, it builds them into MP4 videos (setting “Built-in subtitles”). For other formats on those TVs, choose a version of the video with built-in subtitles.</string>
    <string name="help_hdmi_title">The TV switches input at the end</string>
    <string name="help_hdmi_body">Some TVs go back to their last input (HDMI) when a video ends or is stopped. Universal TV Cast shows its waiting screen on the TV instead, so the next video starts faster. To free the TV, disconnect it.</string>
    <string name="help_sleep_title">Playback stops when the phone screen turns off</string>
    <string name="help_sleep_body">Let Universal TV Cast run in the background.</string>
